;; demos/scenarios/ed-tuesday/config.edn -- ADR-0104, the "A" half of
;; the author's 2026-08-10 "c." ruling (ADR-0103): a scripted single ED
;; shift, weighted toward quick emergent presentations, contrasted
;; against clinic-decade's own population-scale, ambulatory-heavy mix
;; (demos/scenarios/clinic-decade/, ten-year horizon, sparse traffic).
;;
;; The :pathways pool below is genuinely weighted/RNG-drawn -- five
;; scripted ED trajectories, weighted toward the fast/common end (see
;; the per-entry comments; roughly the real-world admission fraction,
;; ~15%, tuned by live-probe below). The ambulatory module tail is
;; authored as a DISJOINT cohort instead of a second weighted pool:
;; ADR-0104's own verification step (`ehrt.sim.run/
;; incompatible-assignments`, `ehrt.sim-engine.assignment/assign-pathway`/
;; `assign-module`) found that a SECOND weighted `:module-assignment`
;; pool would apply to every ordinal the ED pool doesn't explicitly
;; cover -- an RNG-coincident double-assignment (an admission-bearing ED
;; pathway AND a module drawn for the same patient) that the static
;; incompatible-assignment check cannot see coming, since it only
;; reports CERTAIN conflicts. `ModuleAssignment`'s own pool member has
;; no "no module" option (ehrt.patient-simulator.gmf/ModuleAssignment), so
;; a weighted module pool always resolves every uncovered ordinal to
;; SOME module -- there is no way to make it apply to only a minority of
;; patients. The sanctioned shape instead lists the module-tail ordinals
;; explicitly, in BOTH :pathways (an empty override -- the documented
;; module-only-patient pattern, run.clj's own `run` docstring) and
;; :module-assignment (an explicit {:patient-ordinal :module-id}): every
;; ordinal not named here falls through to the ED pool alone, and every
;; named ordinal never draws from it -- conflict-free by construction,
;; not merely undetected by the static check.
;;
;; :facility bumps ONLY the Emergency ward's surge capacity (6 -> 16)
;; versus sim-model/default-facility: with 0 regular ED beds, every ED
;; admission boards on surge immediately (the ladder's own "surge only
;; when full" rule is trivially satisfied at 0 beds), so surge IS the
;; ED's real capacity -- a believable "busy shift" needs more of it than
;; the small-on-purpose default ships. Renal/Cardiology stay at the
;; default 4 beds + 2 surge; this file's own README has the live-probed
;; numbers this pacing (:arrival-gap, the dwell ranges below) was tuned
;; against to hold without capacity exhaustion.
;;
;; :module-horizon-days 90 (~3 months) is short relative to
;; clinic-decade's 3650 (10 years), by this session's own instruction,
;; but a disclosed finding rides along: at THIS thin a tail (8
;; patients), the chosen quick modules (sore_throat/sinusitis/
;; bronchitis/ear_infections) produced ZERO live encounters at every
;; horizon actually tried (14, 90, and even 3650 days -- live-probed,
;; not asserted) with this seed. The mechanism, read from sore_throat's
;; own source (`components/sim/resources/sim/modules/sore_throat.json`,
;; the `Potential_Infection` state): onset is a monthly-Delay-gated
;; complex_transition at roughly 0.5-1% probability per month, so most
;; of a patient's simulated history -- and most of any short forward
;; window -- produces nothing at all; clinic-decade's own README
;; discloses the same low-incidence shape at population scale (68
;; messages from 200 patients over 10 years). This tail is authored
;; anyway, at low weight, per the driving prompt's own ask for one --
;; see this scenario's own README for the actual witnessed number, not
;; silently retuned to force a nonzero result.
{:facility {:id :general-hospital
            ;; ARC 3B SWEEP 2 (ADR-0174 ruling D1, 2026-08-27):
            ;; `:turnaround-minutes` is the range EACH LEG of the bed
            ;; cycle draws from (dirty -> cleaning, cleaning -> ready),
            ;; so a ward's whole turnaround is twice it. These are the
            ;; shipped class defaults written out explicitly; the cycle
            ;; itself is off unless `:bed-cycle` is set below.
            :wards [{:id :ed :name "Emergency" :beds 0 :surge-slots 16
                     :surge-format "%s-H%02d" :class :ed :turnaround-minutes [5 15]}
                    {:id :renal :name "Renal" :beds 4 :surge-slots 2
                     :surge-format "%s-H%02d" :class :inpatient :turnaround-minutes [15 30]}
                    {:id :cardiology :name "Cardiology" :beds 4 :surge-slots 2
                     :surge-format "%s-H%02d" :class :inpatient :turnaround-minutes [15 30]}]}
 :arrival-gap 30
 :pathways [;; Walk-in, quick turnaround -- the ED's own bread and butter.
            {:pathway {:name "ed-fast-track"
                       :steps [{:type :admission :location "Emergency" :reason "Minor laceration"}
                               {:type :delay :from 30 :to 90}
                               {:type :discharge}]}
             :weight 55}
            ;; Worked up, one lab panel, then home.
            {:pathway {:name "ed-observe-and-discharge"
                       :steps [{:type :admission :location "Emergency" :reason "Chest pain, low risk"}
                               {:type :delay :from 60 :to 120}
                               {:type :order :profile :cbc}
                               {:type :delay :from 60 :to 150}
                               {:type :discharge}]}
             :weight 30}
            ;; Admitted, short inpatient dwell.
            {:pathway {:name "ed-admit-renal"
                       :steps [{:type :admission :location "Emergency" :reason "Acute kidney injury"}
                               {:type :delay :from 45 :to 120}
                               {:type :transfer :location "Renal"}
                               {:type :delay :from 240 :to 960}
                               {:type :discharge}]}
             :weight 8}
            {:pathway {:name "ed-admit-cardiology"
                       :steps [{:type :admission :location "Emergency" :reason "Chest pain, high risk"}
                               {:type :delay :from 45 :to 120}
                               {:type :transfer :location "Cardiology"}
                               {:type :delay :from 240 :to 960}
                               {:type :discharge}]}
             :weight 5}
            ;; The rare, resource-heavy trauma case -- imaging in the ED,
            ;; then admitted for the longest dwell in the pool.
            {:pathway {:name "ed-trauma-imaging-admit"
                       :steps [{:type :admission :location "Emergency" :reason "Multi-system trauma"}
                               {:type :delay :from 30 :to 90}
                               {:type :order :profile :cbc}
                               {:type :delay :from 45 :to 90}
                               {:type :transfer :location "Renal"}
                               {:type :delay :from 480 :to 1440}
                               {:type :discharge}]}
             :weight 2}
            ;; The ambulatory module tail -- disjoint from the ED pool
            ;; above by explicit ordinal, per this file's own header
            ;; comment. Empty pathway: the module's own compiled content
            ;; is the whole of this patient's queue (run.clj's own
            ;; module-only-patient pattern).
            {:patient-ordinal 6 :pathway {:name "module-only" :steps []}}
            {:patient-ordinal 18 :pathway {:name "module-only" :steps []}}
            {:patient-ordinal 30 :pathway {:name "module-only" :steps []}}
            {:patient-ordinal 42 :pathway {:name "module-only" :steps []}}
            {:patient-ordinal 54 :pathway {:name "module-only" :steps []}}
            {:patient-ordinal 66 :pathway {:name "module-only" :steps []}}
            {:patient-ordinal 78 :pathway {:name "module-only" :steps []}}
            {:patient-ordinal 90 :pathway {:name "module-only" :steps []}}]
 :modules ["sore_throat" "sinusitis" "bronchitis" "ear_infections"]
 :module-assignment [{:patient-ordinal 6 :module-id "sore_throat"}
                      {:patient-ordinal 18 :module-id "sinusitis"}
                      {:patient-ordinal 30 :module-id "bronchitis"}
                      {:patient-ordinal 42 :module-id "ear_infections"}
                      {:patient-ordinal 54 :module-id "sore_throat"}
                      {:patient-ordinal 66 :module-id "sinusitis"}
                      {:patient-ordinal 78 :module-id "bronchitis"}
                      {:patient-ordinal 90 :module-id "ear_infections"}]
 ;; ARC 3A PART 4 (ADR-0173 ruling D1's commit 2, 2026-08-26): the
 ;; demographic fold, TURNED ON. This is the opt-in -- `:persons` absent
 ;; is the byte-identical path, and this file is one of the six corpora
 ;; that stop taking it.
 ;;
 ;; `:count` IS TWICE THE ARRIVAL COUNT, and both the shape and the
 ;; factor are measurements rather than preferences. With `:persons`
 ;; present an arrival BINDS to a person, and a repeat arrival of
 ;; somebody already registered queues NOTHING (ADR-0173's own first
 ;; tabled deviation: a second `:admission` for a discharged patient
 ;; violates `admission-only-when-new`). So a small pool silently
 ;; deletes clinical content -- probed at `:count` 50 against 100
 ;; arrivals, only 50 patients registered and three quarters of this
 ;; scenario's own module walks never happened.
 ;;
 ;; One person per arrival is not enough either, and the generator-side
 ;; COVERAGE GATE is what said so: at `:count` 100 the birthday paradox
 ;; still makes better than a third of the arrivals repeats, and the
 ;; module coverage that thinned took this scenario's only
 ;; module-compiled `:admission`/`:discharge` pair with it --
 ;; `gated-runs-collectively-produce-every-emittable-event-type` went
 ;; red on exactly those two kinds. At twice the arrival count the
 ;; collision rate drops enough that both come back.
 ;;
 ;; `:years` IS TWENTY, not the ten `ehrt.sim.run/default-person-years`
 ;; would give, and that is a measurement too: at 10 years this
 ;; population produced ZERO occupational injuries (the hazard is
 ;; conditioned on `:employed` person-years, and most of a ten-year walk
 ;; is not), so the clinical hook would have been declared and never
 ;; witnessed. At 20 it fires. A run's own log therefore extends to the
 ;; PERSON horizon, well past its clinical content -- that is what a
 ;; demographic timeline is, and this scenario's own README carries the
 ;; re-witnessed figures.
 :persons {:count 200 :years 20}

 ;; ARC 3B SWEEP 1 (ADR-0174 rulings A1/E1, 2026-08-26): the ENCOUNTER
 ;; HORIZON, LIFTED. This is the opt-in -- `:encounters` absent is the
 ;; byte-identical path, and this file is one of the six corpora that
 ;; stop taking it.
 ;;
 ;; IT CHANGES WHAT THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE MEANS, and that is said here
 ;; rather than left for a reader to notice. A repeat arrival no longer
 ;; queues NOTHING: with no encounter open it opens a SECOND one, on the
 ;; same patient and the same MRN, which is what an MPI under test needs
 ;; to see. So the pool-sizing argument above is no longer about
 ;; DELETED clinical content -- the content a collision used to cost is
 ;; now a return visit -- but the sizing itself is kept unchanged, for
 ;; two reasons: the coverage gate it was tuned against still reads the
 ;; same population, and re-tuning a pool in the same commit that lifts
 ;; the horizon would leave any corpus delta with two possible causes.
 ;;
 ;; WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: a repeat arrival landing while the patient's
 ;; first encounter is still OPEN still opens nothing (`decide
 ;; :repeat-arrival`), and one landing on a `:merged` or `:expired`
 ;; patient still opens nothing either. Both terminals stay absorbing.
 :encounters true

 ;; ARC 3B SWEEP 2 (ADR-0174 section 2(c) plus ruling C, 2026-08-27): the
 ;; BED-STATUS CYCLE, ON. A bed is no longer free the instant its
 ;; occupant leaves it -- it goes `:dirty`, then `:cleaning`, then
 ;; `:ready`, at the per-leg pace each ward's own
 ;; `:turnaround-minutes` above declares, and `allocate` is gated on
 ;; `:ready`.
 ;;
 ;; WHAT IT CHANGES HERE, said rather than left to be noticed. Every
 ;; bed-ready transfer this scenario produced used to fire in the SAME
 ;; SECOND as the discharge that vacated the bed; none does now -- each
 ;; sits at its bed's own READY event instead, decided against the board
 ;; as it stands then. A fresh admission can no longer take a bed
 ;; somebody left moments earlier either. Boarders therefore wait
 ;; longer, which is the point and not a regression.
 ;;
 ;; THE PACING ABOVE IS UNCHANGED, deliberately. Effective capacity
 ;; falls under the cycle and this scenario's own header says its pacing
 ;; was tuned to hold "without capacity exhaustion"; it was re-probed at
 ;; the turnaround this file ships and still holds, so re-tuning it in
 ;; the same commit that lands the cycle would leave any corpus delta
 ;; with two possible causes.
 :bed-cycle true

 ;; ARC 3B SWEEP 3 (ADR-0174 section 2(b) plus ruling C, 2026-08-27):
 ;; SCHEDULING, ON. Arrivals split scheduled-vs-walk-in, appointments
 ;; carry their own outcome, and a discharge may book a return visit.
 ;;
 ;; :scheduled-fraction IS LOW HERE ON PURPOSE, and it is the one
 ;; sub-key this scenario genuinely disagrees with clinic-decade about.
 ;; An ED SHIFT IS WALK-IN TRAFFIC. A scheduled ED arrival is a direct
 ;; admit or an expected transfer -- real, and a minority. 0.15 says
 ;; roughly one arrival in seven walked in with a booking already made;
 ;; clinic-decade's ambulatory decade says 0.70 for exactly the opposite
 ;; reason. ADR-0174 section 2(b) names the six sub-keys and states NO
 ;; VALUES for any of them, so every number here is this session's own
 ;; choice, made on clinical grounds and disclosed as a choice rather
 ;; than cited as a default that does not exist.
 ;;
 ;; THE CAPACITY MARGIN, MEASURED BEFORE THE OPT-IN AND AFTER IT.
 ;; `:exhausted` HALTS a run -- `run-command` surfaces
 ;; :error :capacity-exhausted with no corpus and no self-check at all,
 ;; not the visible rejection ADR-0174 section 2(c) item 4 assumed (that
 ;; premise is WITHDRAWN in the ADR's own 2(c) ratifications). So this
 ;; opt-in owes a measured margin, and the margin that matters is the
 ;; union of beds the four rungs can reach, never one ward's own count:
 ;; Renal peaks at 6 of its own 6 beds non-ready both before and after,
 ;; and is not exhausted either time because rungs 3 and 4 still have
 ;; beds. Against that union, worst home ward at seed 202:
 ;;
 ;;   before  Renal 7 of 26 free (26.9%)   Emergency 7 of 24 (29.2%)
 ;;   after   Renal 10 of 26 free (38.5%)  Emergency 10 of 24 (41.7%)
 ;;
 ;; SCHEDULING LOOSENS THIS SCENARIO RATHER THAN TIGHTENING IT, which
 ;; is the opposite of what "scheduling adds arrivals" predicts and is
 ;; recorded because it is not obvious: cancels and no-shows REMOVE
 ;; admissions, lead times SPREAD arrivals off their original instants,
 ;; and a follow-up produces an `:outpatient-visit`, which occupies no
 ;; bed at all. The ladder reaches rung 3 once (was three times) and
 ;; never rung 4.
 ;;
 ;; THE PACING ABOVE IS UNCHANGED, for the same reason sweep 2 left it
 ;; unchanged: re-tuning in the commit that lands the mechanism would
 ;; leave any corpus delta with two possible causes.
 :scheduling {:scheduled-fraction 0.15
              :lead-time-days [1 3]
              :no-show-rate 0.10
              :reschedule-rate 0.08
              :cancel-rate 0.07
              :follow-up {:rate 0.25 :interval-days [7 30]}}
 :module-horizon-days 90

 ;; ARC 4 SWEEP 2 (ADR-0175 design (a), rulings B1/C1/E1, 2026-08-28):
 ;; RE-STATEMENT CHATTER, ON. A demographic change, a coverage change
 ;; and a registration each now reach the wire as a message of their
 ;; own, and open encounters are periodically re-stated.
 ;;
 ;; THE THREE EVENT RATES ARE 1.0 BECAUSE THAT IS WHAT A REAL INTERFACE
 ;; DOES: an ADT feed sends an update for EVERY demographic change and
 ;; an A28 for every registration. A rate below 1 models an interface
 ;; that drops some, which is a thing worth being able to express and
 ;; not the sensible default. The trigger is DERIVED, never configured:
 ;; A08 when the change happened inside an open encounter, A31 when it
 ;; did not, A28 for a registration.
 ;;
 ;; MEASURED HERE, seed 202 x 100 patients: 2 A08 and 263 A31 from the
 ;; event-driven half. It is 99.2% A31, and that is the modelled world
 ;; rather than a defect -- the person process walks twenty years while
 ;; this scenario's clinical content is one ED shift, so demographic
 ;; churn happens almost entirely BETWEEN encounters.
 ;;
 ;; :rate-per-patient-day IS DELIBERATELY SPARSE HERE, and it is the one
 ;; sub-key this scenario genuinely disagrees with clinic-decade about.
 ;; The periodic census is one draw per patient-day of OPEN-ENCOUNTER
 ;; care, and this scenario's census is dominated by a single stay:
 ;; measured, ONE encounter -- reinstated by a `:cancel-discharge` and
 ;; never re-closed -- is open for 1,432 days at seed 202 and 6,144 days
 ;; at the demo seed 20260811, out of 1,608 and 6,323 patient-days
 ;; respectively. That is 89% and 97% of the whole census on one
 ;; patient. clinic-decade's worst is 141 of 541 (26%), which is why it
 ;; ships 0.25 and this file ships 0.02: at a rate that makes
 ;; clinic-decade's census sensible, this corpus would be one patient's
 ;; A08s several times over.
 ;;
 ;; THE STAY ITSELF IS GROUND TRUTH AND IS NOT THIS SWEEP'S TO FIX (arc
 ;; 4 changes no ground truth at all). A reinstated discharge that is
 ;; never re-issued leaves an encounter open forever, and the census is
 ;; faithfully restating a patient the modelled hospital believes is
 ;; still admitted.
 :chatter {:demographic-update 1.0
           :coverage-change 1.0
           :registered 1.0
           :restatement {:rate-per-patient-day 0.02}}

 ;; ARC 4 SWEEP 2 (ADR-0175 design (c), ruling B1, 2026-08-28): DFT^P03
 ;; CHARGES, ON. Every encounter close now also renders a financial
 ;; message carrying one FT1 per chargeable fact of that encounter.
 ;;
 ;; THE PRICES ARE INVENTED AND THE ENGINE NEVER READS THEM. They are
 ;; emission config, exactly like `:site-profile` and `:latency`: no
 ;; invariant reads a charge, `:charges` reaches no member of
 ;; `ehrt.sim-engine.config/config-keys`, and ground truth is
 ;; byte-identical with and without this block (proved per commit by
 ;; `bin/ground-truth-bracket`, not asserted here). They are round
 ;; plausible numbers chosen to be obviously synthetic; nothing in this
 ;; repository claims they resemble any real chargemaster.
 ;;
 ;; THE TABLE IS DELIBERATELY PARTIAL. `ehrt.sim-emit-hl7.planners/
 ;; plan-charges` COUNTS a code it cannot price and renders no line for
 ;; it, and a partial table is what keeps that census non-vacuous: the
 ;; four codes below cover the CBC order and the three commonest
 ;; procedures across these corpora, and the fourteen rarer procedure
 ;; codes the vendored modules produce are left unpriced on purpose.
 ;;
 ;; `ROOM-BOARD` is the one key no log fact carries a code for --
 ;; `ehrt.sim-emit-hl7.registry/room-and-board-code`, the reserved key
 ;; for the per-inpatient-day line. Absent from this table it would be a
 ;; counted skip like any other; there is no default price anywhere.
 :charges {:price-table {"58410-2"   {:amount 148.00 :display "CBC panel"}
                         "117015009" {:amount 62.00 :display "Throat culture"}
                         "23426006"  {:amount 210.00 :display "Respiratory function measurement"}
                         "441550005" {:amount 88.00 :display "Urinalysis with reflex to culture"}
                         "ROOM-BOARD" {:amount 1875.00 :display "Room and board, per day"}}}

 ;; ARC 4 SWEEP 3 (ADR-0175 design (b), ruling B1, 2026-08-28): STATUS
 ;; LADDERS, ON. Between an order and its result the emitter now
 ;; restates the order's progress -- one ORM^O01 carrying ORC-5 a
 ;; quarter of the way through the wait, one ORU^R01 carrying OBR-25 and
 ;; OBX-11 at the halfway mark -- and the RESULT message itself gains
 ;; the final F in those two fields.
 ;;
 ;; THE FRACTIONS ARE A CLINICAL CHOICE AND ARE DISCLOSED AS ONE. A real
 ;; lab feed's status traffic has no canonical cadence; two rungs per
 ;; order is the smallest ladder that is a ladder, and putting the ORM
 ;; rung first (0.25) and the ORU rung second (0.5) tells the sequence a
 ;; reader expects: the order is picked up, then a preliminary result
 ;; exists, then the final. A site wanting the analyzer chatter of a
 ;; high-volume lab adds fractions; a site wanting none removes the key
 ;; and gets today's bytes back exactly.
 ;;
 ;; NO DRAW, and no ground truth. A rung is a fixed fraction of an
 ;; interval BOTH ends of which are already in the log
 ;; (`:result-available` carries `:order-event-id`), so `plan-ladders`
 ;; takes no RNG at all, claims no `:emission` id-tag, and cannot
 ;; reshuffle anything. `:ladders` reaches no member of
 ;; `ehrt.sim-engine.config/config-keys`.
 ;;
 ;; THE STATUS CODES ARE SITE-PROFILE DATA, NOT A CITATION. HL7 tables
 ;; 0038/0123/0085 appear in no jar and no resource in this tree, so
 ;; `ehrt.sim-emit-hl7.site-profile` ships them as authored, overridable
 ;; code tables beside `:bed-status`. See `docs/site-profiles.md`.
 :ladders {:rungs [0.5] :order-rungs [0.25]}
 ;; ARC 4 SWEEP 4 (ADR-0175 ruling B1, 2026-08-28): SIU. Scheduling's
 ;; four kinds have been in this corpus's GROUND TRUTH since arc 3b
 ;; sweep 3 -- `:scheduling` above is what makes them -- and have
 ;; reached no consumer reading the wire since. This key closes that
 ;; gap, and it is the only arc 4 key that renders an event the log
 ;; ALREADY HELD rather than a restatement of one.
 ;;
 ;; `{}` IS ON, and here alone among the four emission keys. `:chatter`,
 ;; `:charges` and `:ladders` carry the settings that make them do
 ;; anything, so their empty map is off; `:siu` has nothing to
 ;; configure but WHICH KINDS, so the key's presence is the opt-in and
 ;; `:triggers [...]` narrows it. A site wanting only no-show
 ;; notifications writes `{:triggers [:no-show]}`; a site wanting none
 ;; removes the key and gets today's bytes back exactly.
 ;;
 ;; NO DRAW, no planner, and no ground truth: `:siu` decides only
 ;; whether `event->messages` fills a lane-0 slot for an event that
 ;; already exists. It claims no `:emission` id-tag and reaches no
 ;; member of `ehrt.sim-engine.config/config-keys`.
 ;;
 ;; SCH-25'S STATUS CODES ARE SITE-PROFILE DATA, NOT A CITATION, the
 ;; same standing the three ladder tables have: HL7 Table 0278 appears
 ;; in no jar and no resource in this tree. See `docs/site-profiles.md`.
 :siu {}

 ;; ARC 4 SWEEP 5 (ADR-0175 design (f), ruling B1, 2026-08-28):
 ;; `:fan-out` is the subscriber table -- one spool per named
 ;; subscriber under this corpus's own root, written from a FILTER over
 ;; the message vector this run already produced.
 ;;
 ;; IT IS THE ONLY ARC-4 KEY THAT MOVES NO BYTE OF THIS CORPUS.
 ;; `:chatter`, `:charges`, `:ladders` and `:siu` each ADDED messages
 ;; when they were turned on, and each owed a declared digest change
 ;; for doing it. This one adds none and moves none: every message a
 ;; subscriber receives is a message `out/scenarios/<this>/msg-NNN.hl7`
 ;; already holds, at the position it already held, with at most the
 ;; four MSH routing fields rewritten. Turning it on changes what is
 ;; ALSO written, never what is written.
 ;;
 ;; The two feeds below are ADR-0175 section 2(f)'s own worked example.
 ;; `:msh` fills MSH-5/MSH-6, which section 1(ii) measured BLANK on
 ;; every message this project emits under the default profile -- so a
 ;; subscriber's spool is routable on its face without one new field
 ;; position anywhere in the emitter.
 ;;
 ;; A FILTER NAMING A `TYPE^TRIGGER` THIS EMITTER CANNOT PRODUCE IS A
 ;; CONFIG ERROR, rejected before the engine runs. That is deliberate:
 ;; the alternative is a subscriber spool that is silently empty
 ;; forever because somebody wrote `ADT^A05`.
 ;;
 ;; AND ONE RULE WORTH READING BEFORE ADDING A THIRD SUBSCRIBER: a
 ;; `:patient-classes` filter reads PV1-2, and ADT^A20 has no PV1 at
 ;; all. A class filter therefore EXCLUDES every PV1-less message
 ;; unless the subscriber names that trigger explicitly in
 ;; `:message-types`. Stated here rather than left to be discovered as
 ;; a silently empty bed-management feed.
 :fan-out [{:name :adt-feed
            :filter {:message-types #{"ADT^A01" "ADT^A02" "ADT^A03"}}
            :msh {:receiving-app "ADT-CONSUMER" :receiving-facility "WEST"}}
           {:name :lab-feed
            :filter {:message-types #{"ORM^O01" "ORU^R01"}}
            :msh {:receiving-app "LAB-CONSUMER" :receiving-facility "WEST"}}]}
